Yep, I finally did it.  I used a Samurai and ascended with 6.2M
points.  Yay!

Minor spoilers ahead.  Major ones later, after a different ^L.



I managed to get a lot of wishes.  Two from smoky potions, and one from
Vlad's throne.  As a result, I ended up with a lot of artifacts.  They were:

Sting (manufactured), Orcrist (ditto), Mjollnir (wand), Demonbane
(found in Hell), the Tsuguri (quest), Vorpal Blade (potion), Magicbane
(wand), Frost Brand (throne), Excalibur (fountain).

I killed a lot of gray dragons.  However, none of them left scales.  I
got scales from a red dragon and made armor out of that with an
enchant armor scroll.

I figured out most of the interesting things by reading fortune
cookies and talking to the oracle.  She explains right up front how to
win the game.

I was very, VERY careful in this game.  I made sure I had all the
goodies before attempting the endgame.  The armor was speed boots
(wand), GDSM (wand), gauntlets of power (wand), +4 Hawaiian shirt
(shop), Elven cloak (killed poor innocent elf), shield of reflection
(potion), and (believe it or not) an orcish helm.  I also had +2 ring
of protection and 7 protection points from priests and praying.  All
told, the AC was -30.

I also had all the intrinsics.  And used them.

Ok, here's where I give strategy tips that may well ruin your fun.



Do talk to the Oracle.  She always seems to be there, and her advice
is well worth the money.  She doesn't seem to repeat herself much,
either.

Frost Brand is still the most effective weapon in most cases, once you
get it enchanted to +6.  It seems to average about 27 points of damage
per hit.

Some very high level demons (and the wizard) will teleport away from
you when they get hurt.  Since Vorpal Blade can kill instantly, I find
it more useful.  Mjollnir is useful to keep around in case you run
into a cold-resistant creature.  The Tsuguri does huge piles of damage
(didn't check exactly how much) and nobody's resistant.
Unfortunately, it requires two-handed wielding.  When your other armor
is good enough (no shield), this is a good weapon.  It also has an
instant death feature on small and man-sized creatures.  It cuts them
in half!

Vorpal Blade is fun, since it kills Jabberwocks instantly.  I think
the game should print out "Snicker Snack!" when you attack a
Jabberwock.

I had to laugh when I sliced a killer bee in half.  Ginsu Samurai!

A stethescope is useful for finding out how much damage your weapons
are doing.

I did a lot of genociding, and that helped a lot.  I took out Gremlins
first, since they steal intrinsics.  After that, Liches (all) since
they tend to curse items.  Some also have that annoying teleport away
feature.  Then Dragons, because they're so damn hard to kill.  They
seem much tougher than in 3.0.  Then Vampires, because I got tired of
pulling out my Excalibir to avoid level drains.  I was tempted to get
Minautaurs, due to the massive damage, but left them in for the food
and wands they give you.

One level in Hell I really liked.  It was a ghost town, with abandoned
shops and a temple.  Some of the walls had fallen down, and were
replaced by boulders.  Unfortunately, this level has a news monster
called a "shade" which steals intrinsics....  One of them hitting me
stole my speed intrinsic.  I got it back by eating a Quantum Mechanic,
but they might be worth genociding just for that stunt.

Reflection seems to be critical in the first half of the game.  If you
haven't found one already, get a shield of reflection at your first
wish.  Then, a good weapon.  Then, the GDSM.  Then, the speed boots.
Once your armor class is good enough, wand and dragon blasts will
always miss.  However, reflection still helps on the very last level.

Two tools are really useful.  You get blinded, stunned, damaged,
and confused constantly.  A unicorn horn will now fix all these
problems.  If a stat (like strength) goes down, a unicorn horn will
fix it.  This means you can eat poisonous things to get poison
resistance intrinsic.  However, a unicorn corpse always seems to give
the intrinsic all by itself.  Because of the horn, the restore ability
potions are now nearly useless.  Horns cure illness, so make sure you
have one before entering Hell.  You'll find out why.

A tinning kit is amazingly useful.  Tinning a Troll corpse will keep
it from coming back, if you have enough food.  However, you can keep
eating the same troll for a long time!  Since giant corpses will
increase your strength, but also satiate you (making eating too many
dangerous), you should tin the corpses.  Eating from a tin still gives
you the extra strength, but you can keep the tin around for a long
time without it rotting.  However, tins don't seem to have much food
value.

There are a lot of humans you need to kill, like certain preistesses
and Croesus.  When you do, you lose your ESP intrinsic.  A helm of
telepathy would have saved me a lot of trouble, but I was out of
wishes.  Whenever you get a floating eye corpse, tin it.  That way, if
you need to kill a human, you can get your ESP back.

SAVE YOUR CANDLES.  They are very rare, and you need seven of them to
finish the game.  'Nuff said.  "Apply" them to use them in the proper
way, when you've figured it out.

Vlad's tower is much harder to find.  On some level of Hell, there are
two "up" staircases.  One of them leads to the tower.  You have to
search carefully for it.

I didn't find a polymorph wand until I raided the tower.  By that
time, I had all the stuff I needed.  So, I finished the game without
polypiling.  It is possible.

There's a luckstone at the bottom of the Gnomish mines.  It's a very
big help in finishing the game.  Once you get your luck fully charged,
praying will usually result in protection, spellbooks, or the like.
That stone is very hard to find, though.  It is worth the trip to the
bottom.  It doesn't seem to help with Succubi any more, though.

"Elbereth" is now enscribed under the wand of wishing in the castle.
You no longer need to worry about some monster grabbing it.

When you attack the priest at the bottom of Hell, the altar starts
shooting lightning bolts at you!

A wand of secret door detection is very handy for some of the special
levels.

Your first wish with the wand should be for a magic marker.  Use that
to manufacture two charging scrolls.  One for the wand, and one for
the marker!

When you get to the endgame, make sure you bring two items:  a pickaxe
and a ring of levitation.  No, a wand of digging isn't good enough.
Make sure you are fire resistant, too.  A ring of conflict is very
handy, but doesn't make it easy.  A ring of regeneration might help,
too.

The Amulet makes you real hungry, real fast.  Make sure you have a
TON of food ready before grabbing the Amulet.  Trying to cast spells
while holding the Amulet makes you hungry even faster.  Stack the food
by staircases, preferably in bags.

Before grabbing the Amulet, I used a pickaxe to clear a path through
every level of Hell between the staircases.  This sped things up a
lot when running away.  Also, there are a lot of wands of
teleportation in the game.  Save up a bunch of them and use them to
get to the "up" staircase.

There seemed to be disturbingly many wands of death.  I saved them.
One zap finished off my enemy in the quest.  After that, I didn't use
them except to kill the Wizard.  His AC is pretty good, so I usually
missed.  You get more than one chance to hit him if you can arrange a
narrow corridor to bounce the ray at him multiple times.  My favorite
wand I got was from a demon that shot at me with it, missed, and died
when the beam bounced!

Bags of holding are wonderful!  They can carry important items without
fear of them getting cursed, erased, burnt, evaporated, or frozen.
When you bless them, they are lighter.  I put all gems, gold, potions,
scrolls, and spellbooks straight in.  You move faster, since you
aren't burdened.  Whatever you do, though, NEVER put a wand of
cancellation in a bag of holding.

Between the Amulet, the Wizard, and all the Priests, a lot of items
got cursed at the end.  If you bless your important stuff first, it
will go to "normal" before it gets cursed.  Keep really critical stuff
in bags.  I made up a couple blessed scrolls of remove curse.  These
will fix everything in your pack.

The way to get out of the four elemental levels at the end is to find
a magic portal, and it can be ANYWHERE on the level.  You really have
to look for it.  On one level, a monster stepped on it, so I knew
where it was.  On the others, though, I had to search for a LONG time.
On the water level, I had on a ring of levitation and an amulet of
water breathing, so I didn't stay in the "wandering bubble".  I don't
know which one let me get away with it, though.  The portal was
generally on the other side of the screen from where I started, on all
four levels.

Vlad's Tower and magic portals aren't the only tough things to find.
There is a spot of "vibrating ground" on the bottom level that you
have to find.  Search hard for it.

There were a lot of healing potions about.  Drinking one when you're
at max hits gives an extra hit point.  I seem to get most of my (420)
hit points that way.  You'll need all the hit points you can get at
the end of the game (although 250 would probably have been plenty).
Your levels max out at 30, but Wraith corpses and gain level potions
still give you 5 hit points apiece.

I identified a lot of gems at the end.  They boosted my score
considerably.  Given the difficulty of the end-game, you still need a
lot of armor, weapons, and wands, and it is a good idea to stay
unencumbered, so perhaps I didn't get as many gems as I wanted....

The Amulet seems to give certain powers when you are holding it.  I
never did try it on (wanted to keep lifesaving amulet on), but the
Oracle says that it does something interesting.

You aren't the only monster with reflection anymore!

The Amiga version seems prone to crashes.  However, if I reboot and
run the game immediately, it seems fine.  I can only conclude that it
has a memory fragmentation problem.

I figured out how to make holy water from reading a fortune cookie!

I wished for a Horn of Plenty so I wouldn't have to carry food.  It
didn't help much.  It game me mostly tripe rations (70% tripe, or so),
and quickly ran out of charges.  Total waste of a wish.

Well, that's enough spoilers for now.  I can get back to my real life.
You are all doomed to continue playing, but I am finally free!

Happy Hacking!

